He actually built a section of SUB,

262
00:14:17,201 --> 00:14:20,740
put in the reactor of exactly the type that would go in Nautilus,

263
00:14:20,741 --> 00:14:23,580
and ran it for 1,000 hours, ran it for the equivalent

264
00:14:23,581 --> 00:14:25,620
of a trip back and forth across the Atlantic.

265
00:14:25,621 --> 00:14:26,660
And it worked.

266
00:14:26,661 --> 00:14:29,120
And the payoff was that every time they made a change

267
00:14:29,121 --> 00:14:31,560
in the test reactor, they could make it back in the Nautilus,

268
00:14:31,561 --> 00:14:33,420
because they were identical.

269
00:14:33,421 --> 00:14:35,700
And that was a major reason why Nautilus happened

270
00:14:35,701 --> 00:14:38,240
as fast as it did.

271
00:14:38,241 --> 00:14:41,040
Protecting the crew from lethal radioactivity

272
00:14:41,041 --> 00:14:42,840
was another problem.

273
00:14:42,841 --> 00:14:46,040
Now, a man can walk over the shield and through the ship,

274
00:14:46,041 --> 00:14:48,980
and he will not be irradiated dangerously

275
00:14:48,981 --> 00:14:50,780
over a long period of time.

276
00:14:50,781 --> 00:14:53,380
You had to put enough shielding in the boat

277
00:14:53,381 --> 00:14:57,160
to protect the people, and that made the boat heavy.

278
00:14:57,161 --> 00:14:58,960
And remember, a submarine's a blimp.

279
00:14:58,961 --> 00:15:03,700
The heavier it goes, it is, the bigger it's got to be,

280
00:15:03,701 --> 00:15:08,440
so that it'll float. The engineers created a workable

281
00:15:08,441 --> 00:15:11,640
ratio between the lead shielding and the boat's size.

282
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:19,880
On January 21, 1954, the world's first nuclear submarine was

283
00:15:19,881 --> 00:15:24,220
launched. It was an extraordinary

284
00:15:24,221 --> 00:15:27,780
technological machine. The nuclear reactor powered the

285
00:15:27,781 --> 00:15:31,240
boat using heat provided by nuclear fission, the splitting

286
00:15:31,241 --> 00:15:34,740
atoms. The reactor's heat was transferred

287
00:15:34,741 --> 00:15:37,880
to water, which circulated through a primary piping loop

288
00:15:37,881 --> 00:15:43,780
that also included the reactor, pumps, and the steam generator.

289
00:15:43,781 --> 00:15:47,380
A secondary loop sent steam from the steam generator to a turbine,

290
00:15:47,381 --> 00:15:51,000
which supplied the vessel with electricity, and to the main

291
00:15:51,001 --> 00:15:54,400
propulsion turbine that drove the propeller.

292
00:15:54,401 --> 00:15:57,900
The steam was condensed into water and recirculated back

293
00:15:57,901 --> 00:15:59,700
back into the system.

294
00:15:59,800 --> 00:16:03,100
No air or oxygen was required to run the reactor,

295
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:05,400
allowing a sub to operate independently

296
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:08,540
from the Earth's atmosphere.

297
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:11,340
You had a true submarine a la Jules Verne.

298
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:13,740
It could submerge, and it did not have to come back up,

299
00:16:13,840 --> 00:16:18,320
and it could stay submerged almost indefinitely.

300
00:16:18,420 --> 00:16:20,420
The endurance of the crew was the only limitation.

301
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:22,060
The idea that the submarine can now

302
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:25,820
employ stealth to maximum effect and is not any longer

303
00:16:25,821 --> 00:16:28,580
any longer driven to the surface by the necessity

304
00:16:28,581 --> 00:16:30,120
to recharge batteries.

305
00:16:30,121 --> 00:16:31,460
That's completely gone.

306
00:16:31,461 --> 00:16:35,960
And for submariners, that was the ultimate rush.

307
00:16:35,961 --> 00:16:38,160
You must remember that although there had been fast subs

308
00:16:38,161 --> 00:16:41,520
before, there had never been a sustained high-speed sub.

309
00:16:41,521 --> 00:16:45,620
No one had the faintest idea of what, say, 23 knots was like

310
00:16:45,621 --> 00:16:47,620
on a sustained basis.

311
00:16:47,621 --> 00:16:52,320
It turned out that she could evade any anti-submarine force.

312
00:16:52,321 --> 00:16:58,020
On January 17, 1955, the boat began its first extended sea

313
00:16:58,021 --> 00:17:02,380
trials, which were spectacularly successful.

314
00:17:02,381 --> 00:17:07,220
The captain sent a radio message underway on nuclear power.

315
00:17:07,221 --> 00:17:11,020
It was the beginning of a new and dangerous era.

316
00:17:11,021 --> 00:17:14,220
Soon after, the USS Seawolf was launched.

317
00:17:14,221 --> 00:17:16,520
And within three years, the USS Skate

318
00:17:16,521 --> 00:17:18,880
was the first sub to break through the ice at the North

319
00:17:18,881 --> 00:17:20,660
Pole.

320
00:17:20,661 --> 00:17:27,060
Their missions were espionage, anti-sub and anti-ship warfare.

321
00:17:27,061 --> 00:17:30,900
Then in 1958, the Soviets, under the direction of Admiral

322
00:17:30,901 --> 00:17:34,400
Sergei Gorshkov, launched their first series of nuclear

323
00:17:34,401 --> 00:17:40,340
submarines, Hotel, Echo, and November, from the Sevmash

324
00:17:40,341 --> 00:17:43,040
Boatyard near Archangel in the Arctic.

325
00:17:43,041 --> 00:17:46,760
All Soviet sub names and classes were designated by NATO for

326
00:17:46,761 --> 00:17:50,660
reference by the West. They were very noisy.

327
00:17:50,661 --> 00:17:53,460
They were pretty fast, and they were unsafe for the crews.

328
00:17:53,461 --> 00:17:54,860
A lot of sailors in those submarines

329
00:17:54,861 --> 00:17:58,860
died over the succeeding years of radiation poisoning

330
00:17:58,861 --> 00:18:02,540
because the Russians did not invest the same money or effort

331
00:18:02,541 --> 00:18:06,740
in safety for the crews that we did.

332
00:18:06,741 --> 00:18:09,840
Several Soviet subs used a lead-bismuth alloy,

333
00:18:09,841 --> 00:18:11,580
which circulated at high temperatures

334
00:18:11,581 --> 00:18:13,820
through the nuclear reactor.

335
00:18:13,821 --> 00:18:16,120
It allowed for a greater concentration of energy

336
00:18:16,121 --> 00:18:17,320
when heating water.

337
00:18:17,321 --> 00:18:20,380
The US used a simpler pressurized water design,

338
00:18:20,381 --> 00:18:23,720
as did most Soviet subs.

339
00:18:23,721 --> 00:18:25,880
The great thing about pressurized water

340
00:18:25,881 --> 00:18:28,020
was it was inherently very stable.

341
00:18:28,021 --> 00:18:32,560
As the water got hotter, the reactor would slow down a bit.

342
00:18:32,561 --> 00:18:34,720
That meant that it couldn't get out of hand.

343
00:18:34,721 --> 00:18:37,840
The water itself wouldn't carry radioactivity.

344
00:18:37,841 --> 00:18:41,000
With the liquid metal, you carry a lot more heat out.

345
00:18:41,001 --> 00:18:43,040
You can make much hotter steam with it.

346
00:18:43,041 --> 00:18:46,640
On the other hand, that liquid metal does not itself

347
00:18:46,641 --> 00:18:49,300
moderate the reactor.

348
00:18:49,301 --> 00:18:52,360
In one case, the metal didn't melt properly.

349
00:18:52,361 --> 00:18:55,540
They ended up with this solidified radioactive gunk,

350
00:18:55,541 --> 00:18:56,840
and that had cut the sub up.

351
00:18:56,841 --> 00:19:02,680
The problem with liquid metal is that it gets radioactive

352
00:19:02,681 --> 00:19:06,320
and has a very, very long half-life.

353
00:19:06,321 --> 00:19:12,160
So if you have a problem at sea, you can't fix it.

354
00:19:12,161 --> 00:19:14,660
Back at the time Nautilus was being built,

355
00:19:14,661 --> 00:19:18,520
the US created another revolutionary design.

356
00:19:18,521 --> 00:19:22,320
In 1953, the Navy tested a diesel submarine

357
00:19:22,321 --> 00:19:25,560
with a whale-shaped or teardrop hull.

358
00:19:25,561 --> 00:19:28,520
It was able to attain the astonishing speed of more than 30

359
00:19:28,521 --> 00:19:30,540
knots underwater.

360
00:19:30,541 --> 00:19:33,740
Prior to the Albacore hull, submarine hull shapes were

361
00:19:33,741 --> 00:19:36,540
really optimized for surface operation.

362
00:19:36,541 --> 00:19:39,300
They go fast on the surface, and then they submerge and go

363
00:19:39,301 --> 00:19:42,040
slower while they were doing their operations.

364
00:19:42,041 --> 00:19:45,020
Albacore could go faster underwater than it could

365
00:19:45,021 --> 00:19:46,240
on the surface.

366
00:19:46,241 --> 00:19:51,540
The Albacore achieved speeds never before seen in submarines.

367
00:19:51,640 --> 00:19:54,980
Due to its cylindrical shape, water flowed off the Albacore

368
00:19:55,080 --> 00:19:59,980
bow much faster than earlier hull designs.

369
00:20:00,080 --> 00:20:04,000
By the mid-1950s, the US began building a six-boat class of

370
00:20:04,100 --> 00:20:07,300
nuclear submarine called Skipjack.

371
00:20:07,400 --> 00:20:11,940
The class of vessels has always been named after its first boat.

372
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:15,440
They redesigned the power plant to make it a bit more compact.

373
00:20:15,441 --> 00:20:18,440
They combine it with the Skipjack, with the Albacore

374
00:20:18,540 --> 00:20:21,120
hull, and zow!

375
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:24,480
When the USS Skipjack was launched in 1960,

376
00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:28,860
the US now had a nuclear sub of astonishing speed, endurance,

377
00:20:28,960 --> 00:20:31,360
and seemingly endless power.

378
00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:33,720
When her first skipper brought her back into port

379
00:20:33,820 --> 00:20:35,700
after her initial tests, he said,

380
00:20:35,800 --> 00:20:36,960
put a Simonized job on her.

381
00:20:37,060 --> 00:20:39,500
I want to take her home.

382
00:20:39,600 --> 00:20:40,660
She was fast.

383
00:20:40,760 --> 00:20:42,000
She was maneuverable.

384
00:20:42,100 --> 00:20:44,540
She could go deeper than any submarine up to that point

385
00:20:44,541 --> 00:20:49,640
time, and she combined the best the US Navy had to offer.

386
00:20:49,740 --> 00:20:52,440
But these subs were minnows compared to the monsters

387
00:20:52,540 --> 00:20:56,780
being developed. Next, as the Cold War heated up,

388
00:20:56,880 --> 00:20:59,980
the US and the Soviets would create a new breed of nuclear

389
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:03,000
submarine. With such destructive power,

390
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:05,000
it threatened the world.

391
00:21:05,001 --> 00:21:12,000
In the mid-1950s, America began work on a revolutionary concept

392
00:21:12,100 --> 00:21:14,940
for submarine warfare.

393
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:18,940
A feisty visionary leader, Admiral William Raybourn,

394
00:21:19,040 --> 00:21:21,320
was placed in charge of a US program

395
00:21:21,420 --> 00:21:26,360
to launch ballistic missiles from submerged submarines.

396
00:21:26,460 --> 00:21:29,300
But the challenges of launching missiles from underwater subs

397
00:21:29,400 --> 00:21:31,100
seemed insurmountable.

398
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:33,220
The first problem was what it was.

399
00:21:33,221 --> 00:21:35,220
The first problem was water displacement

400
00:21:35,320 --> 00:21:38,220
inside the sub itself.

401
00:21:38,320 --> 00:21:40,460
You have this large object that the submarine suddenly

402
00:21:40,560 --> 00:21:42,760
squirts out, so you have to fill a tank

403
00:21:42,860 --> 00:21:43,960
to compensate for the weight.

404
00:21:44,060 --> 00:21:46,860
Otherwise, the submarine will bounce around.

405
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:49,700
Engineers developed advanced ballast systems

406
00:21:49,800 --> 00:21:53,040
that quickly restored neutral buoyancy after a launch,

407
00:21:53,140 --> 00:21:56,340
allowing the sub to absorb the shock of firing a missile.

408
00:21:56,440 --> 00:21:58,640
Technical problem number two is knowing

409
00:21:58,740 --> 00:22:00,040
the exact position of the submarine

410
00:22:00,140 --> 00:22:03,220
so that you can deliver the missile to its target system.

411
00:22:03,221 --> 00:22:05,680
safely and accurately.

412
00:22:05,780 --> 00:22:07,820
Navigation was significantly improved

413
00:22:07,920 --> 00:22:11,800
with orbiting satellites, which radioed the sub's exact location

414
00:22:11,900 --> 00:22:13,940
to its commanders.

415
00:22:14,040 --> 00:22:15,760
They could then accurately calculate

416
00:22:15,860 --> 00:22:18,040
the distance to a target.

417
00:22:18,140 --> 00:22:20,400
Then computerized missile guidance systems

418
00:22:20,500 --> 00:22:23,540
were installed in the weapons to ensure the correct target

419
00:22:23,640 --> 00:22:24,680
was hit.

420
00:22:24,780 --> 00:22:28,040
But the most dangerous challenge was how to carry and light off

421
00:22:28,140 --> 00:22:31,780
a liquid fuel missile inside a sub.

422
00:22:31,781 --> 00:22:33,620
If you kick over a liquid fuel missile,

423
00:22:33,720 --> 00:22:35,480
you get a big fire if you're lucky,

424
00:22:35,580 --> 00:22:38,820
and you get a big explosion if you're not.

425
00:22:38,920 --> 00:22:42,020
Liquid fuel was the leading propellant of the time.

426
00:22:42,120 --> 00:22:44,520
But it was hard to handle, highly flammable,

427
00:22:44,620 --> 00:22:48,540
and could wreak havoc inside the confines of a submarine.

428
00:22:48,640 --> 00:22:52,340
The big breakthrough that enabled us to do submerged

429
00:22:52,440 --> 00:22:57,280
launch was the advent of solid fuel rockets.

430
00:22:57,380 --> 00:23:00,080
Also, the size of the missile itself,

431
00:23:00,081 --> 00:23:02,680
the dimensions of the missile got much smaller

432
00:23:02,780 --> 00:23:06,780
when we got to solid fuel, which made it easier to put it

433
00:23:06,880 --> 00:23:09,280
in a submarine hull.

434
00:23:09,380 --> 00:23:13,220
Solid fuel was made with a combination of nitrate, carbon,

435
00:23:13,320 --> 00:23:14,720
and sulfur.

436
00:23:14,820 --> 00:23:19,300
It was much safer because it was bonded by plastics, was compact,

437
00:23:19,400 --> 00:23:21,240
and less volatile.

438
00:23:21,340 --> 00:23:24,000
Instead of igniting the missile inside the sub,

439
00:23:24,100 --> 00:23:26,800
the weapon belched out of the hatch with compressed air.

440
00:23:30,080 --> 00:23:33,380
Rocket engines then ignited and propelled the missile up into

441
00:23:33,381 --> 00:23:34,880
the atmosphere.

442
00:23:34,881 --> 00:23:39,380
In December 1959, the US unveiled the USS George

443
00:23:39,381 --> 00:23:42,820
Washington, the first of a five-boat class of the world's

444
00:23:42,821 --> 00:23:46,640
first nuclear ballistic missile submarines, boomers.

445
00:23:50,740 --> 00:23:54,080
It was a lethal sea beast, a killing machine that carried

446
00:23:54,081 --> 00:23:58,980
16 Polaris missiles with a range of 1,000 miles.

447
00:23:58,981 --> 00:24:02,680
Each was topped with a 50-kiloton nuclear warhead.

448
00:24:02,681 --> 00:24:06,320
The Hiroshima bomb was about 15 kilotons.

449
00:24:06,321 --> 00:24:09,320
The Polaris submarines provided the United States

450
00:24:09,321 --> 00:24:12,220
tremendous advantage in terms of protecting

451
00:24:12,221 --> 00:24:14,420
its strategic arsenal.

452
00:24:14,421 --> 00:24:18,440
The submarine at sea submerged was essentially

453
00:24:18,441 --> 00:24:20,040
impossible to find.

454
00:24:20,041 --> 00:24:21,640
It's kind of hard to develop a countermeasure

455
00:24:21,641 --> 00:24:23,180
against something if you don't know where it is.

456
00:24:23,181 --> 00:24:26,340
If you don't know where it is, you can't attack it and kill it.

457
00:24:26,341 --> 00:24:29,540
And so you can't defend yourself against it.

458
00:24:29,640 --> 00:24:32,480
In just over a decade, the US had created

459
00:24:32,580 --> 00:24:36,220
a new breed of sub, able to stay hidden and submerged

460
00:24:36,320 --> 00:24:39,960
for months on end, carrying the largest amount of firepower

461
00:24:40,060 --> 00:24:41,860
known to man.

462
00:24:41,960 --> 00:24:44,660
The mission of the boomers was to take their lethal cargo

463
00:24:44,760 --> 00:24:48,220
and disappear into the shadows of the ocean's vastness

464
00:24:48,320 --> 00:24:52,540
and wait for a command that hopefully would never come.

465
00:24:52,640 --> 00:24:56,340
Submarines give you certainty and uncertainty.

466
00:24:56,341 --> 00:25:01,080
It puts in the mind of the opponent a terrible certainty

467
00:25:01,180 --> 00:25:04,580
as to what you can do and a terrible uncertainty

468
00:25:04,680 --> 00:25:06,820
as to whether you're going to do it or when

469
00:25:06,920 --> 00:25:08,080
or even if you're there.

470
00:25:10,680 --> 00:25:14,200
Throughout the 1960s, the Polaris missiles on subs

471
00:25:14,300 --> 00:25:17,620
became even more deadly by stacking several nuclear war

472
00:25:17,720 --> 00:25:20,660
heads on each, greatly increasing their destructive

473
00:25:20,760 --> 00:25:22,600
power.

474
00:25:22,601 --> 00:25:27,100
In 1970, Poseidon missiles introduced MERVs, or multiple

475
00:25:27,101 --> 00:25:31,200
independently targeted reentry vehicles, that could hit targets

476
00:25:31,201 --> 00:25:35,840
2,500 miles away. After a Poseidon missile completed

477
00:25:35,841 --> 00:25:39,660
its three rocket boost phase, its nose cone would reenter the

478
00:25:39,661 --> 00:25:43,660
atmosphere, and its attached multiple warheads, or MERVs,

479
00:25:43,661 --> 00:25:47,260
would split off and detonate over a wide area.

480
00:25:47,261 --> 00:25:50,500
Each one of the warheads can be independently targeted to

481
00:25:50,501 --> 00:25:54,740
to different cities, locations, industrial sites, military bases.

482
00:25:54,840 --> 00:25:57,600
So with one missile, you can effectively take out multiple

483
00:25:57,700 --> 00:25:58,600
targets.

484
00:25:58,700 --> 00:26:02,540
So it multiplies by so many times the effectiveness of fleet

485
00:26:02,640 --> 00:26:05,040
ballistic missile submarines.

486
00:26:05,140 --> 00:26:08,560
But the Soviet Union refused to be left behind.

487
00:26:08,660 --> 00:26:12,560
In 1967, the Soviets launched what NATO would call the Yankee

488
00:26:12,660 --> 00:26:17,600
Class, a boomer sub with 16 ballistic missiles with a range

489
00:26:17,700 --> 00:26:19,700
of 1,300 miles.

490
00:26:19,701 --> 00:26:23,440
It displaced 9,600 tons and carried larger warheads

491
00:26:23,540 --> 00:26:25,940
than the Polaris missile.

492
00:26:26,040 --> 00:26:30,280
The US countered in 1982 with the Ohio class sub, which

493
00:26:30,380 --> 00:26:35,980
could carry 24 Trident missiles, each with up to eight warheads

494
00:26:36,080 --> 00:26:37,280
of 100 kilotons.

495
00:26:40,720 --> 00:26:43,560
Just one Ohio submarine launching all of its 24

496
00:26:43,660 --> 00:26:48,300
Tridents to a distant continent would unleash 192 separate

497
00:26:48,301 --> 00:26:52,300
thermonuclear explosions, releasing 19,000 kilotons of

498
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:56,940
energy, about 1,300 Hiroshima bombs.

499
00:26:57,040 --> 00:27:00,440
Besides killing millions of people instantly, the explosions

500
00:27:00,540 --> 00:27:03,940
would disperse an estimated 2 million tons of radioactive

501
00:27:04,040 --> 00:27:07,960
debris into the atmosphere, altering weather patterns

502
00:27:08,060 --> 00:27:09,960
and the Earth's climate.

503
00:27:10,060 --> 00:27:14,100
One missile can hit enormous number of targets from a great

504
00:27:14,200 --> 00:27:16,900
distance, like 6,000 miles.

505
00:27:16,901 --> 00:27:20,200
It's quite a remarkable animal.

506
00:27:20,300 --> 00:27:21,700
If you think of it actually being used,

507
00:27:21,800 --> 00:27:24,100
it's quite a horrible animal.

508
00:27:24,200 --> 00:27:27,240
Next, as boomer subs began to proliferate,

509
00:27:27,340 --> 00:27:30,740
the US and Soviets were also pouring billions of dollars

510
00:27:30,840 --> 00:27:33,520
into improving their attack submarines,

511
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:36,860
creating vessels of untold stealth and quiet.

512
00:27:41,460 --> 00:27:45,000
As the Soviet Union began launching more nuclear submarines

513
00:27:45,001 --> 00:27:50,000
in the early 1960s, the US began focusing on anti-submarine

514
00:27:50,100 --> 00:27:52,640
warfare to deal with the emerging threat.

515
00:27:52,740 --> 00:27:55,780
US designers became obsessed with quietness as a means

516
00:27:55,880 --> 00:27:59,120
to secretly shadow, monitor, and if necessary,

517
00:27:59,220 --> 00:28:01,180
take out the new Soviet vessels.

518
00:28:01,280 --> 00:28:03,980
Con sonar, have a weak noise level bearing 298.

519
00:28:06,820 --> 00:28:08,960
Con eyes, sonar.

520
00:28:09,060 --> 00:28:11,400
If your submarine is so loud as making fish jump out

521
00:28:11,500 --> 00:28:13,400
of the water or holding their ears,

522
00:28:13,401 --> 00:28:16,400
and you're not making any noise at all, who's got the advantage?

523
00:28:16,401 --> 00:28:19,400
If you're invisible, the other guy can't hurt you.

524
00:28:19,401 --> 00:28:21,400
If you're invisible and he is visible, you win.

525
00:28:21,401 --> 00:28:23,400
It's that simple.

526
00:28:23,401 --> 00:28:25,400
The Nautilus was hard-mounted machinery,

527
00:28:25,401 --> 00:28:27,400
very little sound isolation.

528
00:28:27,401 --> 00:28:30,400
In that kind of a submarine, the radiated noise

529
00:28:30,401 --> 00:28:36,400
follows almost exactly a curve of speed versus power.

530
00:28:36,401 --> 00:28:39,400
The faster it goes, the more it radiates.

531
00:28:39,401 --> 00:28:44,400
The whole idea with sound quieting is to separate those two.

532
00:28:44,500 --> 00:28:50,400
And what you really want it to do is be quiet across the full speed range.

533
00:28:50,500 --> 00:28:58,400
Rickover said it, and it's true, quieting is a lot more difficult problem than just creating the nuclear reactor plant.

534
00:28:58,500 --> 00:29:03,400
Silencing soon became a religion for U.S. sub-designers.

535
00:29:03,500 --> 00:29:07,400
The way you make a submarine quiet is you look at the noisy part.

536
00:29:07,401 --> 00:29:10,300
That's machinery, mostly geared machinery.

537
00:29:10,400 --> 00:29:13,280
You put it on a rigid platform called a raft.

538
00:29:13,380 --> 00:29:15,340
You separate the raft from the hull.

539
00:29:15,440 --> 00:29:18,080
You put in springs that absorb the sound.

540
00:29:18,180 --> 00:29:21,820
That quiets it down enormously.

541
00:29:21,920 --> 00:29:24,020
The springs came with rubber mounts

542
00:29:24,120 --> 00:29:26,120
that dampened vibrations.

543
00:29:26,220 --> 00:29:30,060
Rubber tiles coating the hull kept all sounds inside.

544
00:29:30,160 --> 00:29:33,100
Every piece of gear was analyzed for lubrication

545
00:29:33,200 --> 00:29:36,100
and better fitting joints and valves.

546
00:29:36,101 --> 00:29:39,340
Noise monitoring systems were put in place to catch errant

547
00:29:39,440 --> 00:29:41,740
sounds from the engine's pumps.

548
00:29:41,840 --> 00:29:45,140
Even kitchen utensils used by cooks in the sub's galley

549
00:29:45,240 --> 00:29:46,940
were scrutinized.

550
00:29:47,040 --> 00:29:51,940
The blades on the mixer are coated with plastic or

551
00:29:52,040 --> 00:29:54,860
rubber, so when they bash into each other, they don't make any

552
00:29:54,960 --> 00:29:55,960
noise.

553
00:29:56,060 --> 00:29:59,560
By the 1960s, new digital technologies

554
00:29:59,660 --> 00:30:03,260
created passive sonar, a heightened ability to pick up

555
00:30:03,261 --> 00:30:07,660
to pick up ocean sounds from hundreds of miles away.

556
00:30:07,760 --> 00:30:10,260
By reducing a sub's ambient noise,

557
00:30:10,360 --> 00:30:13,000
distant signals became clearer.

558
00:30:13,100 --> 00:30:16,200
Computers were then used to enhance, sift, and clarify

559
00:30:16,300 --> 00:30:19,040
the incoming sounds.

560
00:30:19,140 --> 00:30:21,380
In submarine versus submarine warfare,

561
00:30:21,480 --> 00:30:26,520
it is essential that you hear the enemy before he hears you.

562
00:30:26,620 --> 00:30:28,360
There are two ways to ensure that.

563
00:30:28,460 --> 00:30:31,380
One is to have a quieter submarine than he has,

564
00:30:31,381 --> 00:30:35,260
put less energy into the water, and also to have a better sonar

565
00:30:35,360 --> 00:30:39,560
system than he does, to hear him before he hears you.

566
00:30:39,660 --> 00:30:41,620
The new silencing and listening techniques

567
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:44,560
were incorporated into the nuclear attack submarine

568
00:30:44,660 --> 00:30:50,060
USS Thresher, launched in July of 1960.

569
00:30:50,160 --> 00:30:53,140
To improve the passive sonar system in Thresher,

570
00:30:53,240 --> 00:30:56,980
the entire bow was dedicated to sonar rays.

571
00:30:57,080 --> 00:30:59,080
The bow of the submarine is the quietest place

572
00:30:59,081 --> 00:31:04,080
on the submarine, which permitted the sonar to pick up

573
00:31:04,180 --> 00:31:07,080
fainter acoustic energy in the water

574
00:31:07,180 --> 00:31:10,820
than it would have otherwise.

575
00:31:10,920 --> 00:31:14,120
The new sub could locate and shadow Soviet naval forces

576
00:31:14,220 --> 00:31:16,100
virtually unseen.

577
00:31:16,200 --> 00:31:18,840
But then came a tragedy.

578
00:31:18,940 --> 00:31:21,840
With great regret and sadness that this ship,

579
00:31:21,940 --> 00:31:27,400
with 129 fine soles aboard, is lost.

580
00:31:27,401 --> 00:31:32,040
At 7.47 p.m. on April 10, 1963,

581
00:31:32,140 --> 00:31:37,740
Thresher began a deep descent 200 miles off the coast of Boston.

582
00:31:37,840 --> 00:31:43,240
At 9.13 p.m., Thresher radioed that she was having difficulties.

583
00:31:43,340 --> 00:31:46,860
After several minutes, the boat began diving.

584
00:31:46,960 --> 00:31:50,260
Radio operators heard the sub breaking up as it reached crushed

585
00:31:50,360 --> 00:31:52,860
depth.

586
00:31:52,960 --> 00:31:55,300
When you get a hull rupture in a submarine,

587
00:31:55,301 --> 00:31:58,640
the rapid change in air pressure is essentially the reverse

588
00:31:58,740 --> 00:32:00,500
of what happened when they blew the ballast tanks.

589
00:32:00,600 --> 00:32:03,540
The air ignites.

590
00:32:03,640 --> 00:32:07,780
And so everybody is flash-fried in a matter of seconds.

591
00:32:07,880 --> 00:32:10,880
Once the outside water pressure cracked the hull,

592
00:32:10,980 --> 00:32:14,080
the sub's internal atmosphere began to implode,

593
00:32:14,180 --> 00:32:16,220
causing the air and gases to heat up

594
00:32:16,320 --> 00:32:19,320
until there was an explosion.

595
00:32:19,420 --> 00:32:21,660
The destroyed sub settled on the ocean floor

596
00:32:21,661 --> 00:32:25,000
at a depth of 8,000 feet.

597
00:32:25,100 --> 00:32:28,300
A later investigation concluded that internal leaking had led

598
00:32:28,400 --> 00:32:30,640
to the shutdown of the reactor.

599
00:32:30,740 --> 00:32:34,040
The sub then tried to blow her emergency ballast tanks,

600
00:32:34,140 --> 00:32:36,680
but couldn't because of frozen pipes.

601
00:32:36,780 --> 00:32:39,940
Thresher was the most advanced sub we'd ever built,

602
00:32:40,040 --> 00:32:44,680
and it was almost unthinkable that it would sink.

603
00:32:44,780 --> 00:32:46,580
Luckily, Thresher's nuclear reactor

604
00:32:46,680 --> 00:32:49,620
did not blow up from underwater surface pressure,

605
00:32:49,621 --> 00:32:54,560
which exceeded 500,000 pounds per square foot.

606
00:32:54,660 --> 00:32:57,120
The Navy's containment and shielding designs proved

607
00:32:57,220 --> 00:33:00,560
successful, and the reactor remains on the ocean bottom

608
00:33:00,660 --> 00:33:04,560
to this day. Radioactivity at the site

609
00:33:04,660 --> 00:33:08,680
is continually monitored and is said to be minimal.

610
00:33:08,780 --> 00:33:11,580
It survived that depth because of a combination of the

611
00:33:11,680 --> 00:33:15,480
metallurgy, the metal used, the thickness, and the shape.

612
00:33:15,580 --> 00:33:18,580
The closer it is to being spherical, the more survivable

613
00:33:18,581 --> 00:33:21,480
because of the exertion, the way the pressure is distributed

614
00:33:21,580 --> 00:33:24,580
across the face of the containment vessel.

615
00:33:24,680 --> 00:33:28,580
But the accident shook the Navy to its nuclear core.

616
00:33:28,680 --> 00:33:32,580
Soon after, Admiral Hyman Rickover implemented a rigorous

617
00:33:32,680 --> 00:33:35,580
investigation and a torturous inspection system for future

618
00:33:35,680 --> 00:33:38,580
sub-construction. The submarine safety system

619
00:33:38,680 --> 00:33:42,580
introduced a level of detail that had never been seen before.

620
00:33:42,680 --> 00:33:47,580
All wells were inspected. All materials were

621
00:33:47,581 --> 00:33:52,580
were tracked, where they came from, how they were used,

622
00:33:52,680 --> 00:33:56,060
how they were treated, where they went in the submarine.

623
00:33:56,160 --> 00:33:59,020
Next, besides the strict testing and inspection

624
00:33:59,120 --> 00:34:02,060
of materials and machinery, the quest for safety

625
00:34:02,160 --> 00:34:05,660
is also evident in the training at the US Naval Submarine

626
00:34:05,760 --> 00:34:07,200
School in Groton, Connecticut.

627
00:34:14,040 --> 00:34:16,200
The backbone of the US submarine fleet

628
00:34:16,201 --> 00:34:20,040
is the training given enlistees at the Naval Submarine School

629
00:34:20,140 --> 00:34:22,300
in Bruton, Connecticut.

630
00:34:22,400 --> 00:34:26,180
Every submariner must first take classes for about one year

631
00:34:26,280 --> 00:34:30,220
in tactics, navigation, electronics, weapons,

632
00:34:30,320 --> 00:34:32,120
and sonar, among others.

633
00:34:32,220 --> 00:34:36,020
Let's show you how it all fits together.

634
00:34:36,120 --> 00:34:38,320
Underwater is kind of dangerous.

635
00:34:38,420 --> 00:34:40,360
There's a lot of water pressure.

636
00:34:40,460 --> 00:34:42,760
You're in a place with highly radioactive stuff

637
00:34:42,860 --> 00:34:44,420
that can kill you.

638
00:34:44,421 --> 00:34:46,720
It's very important that you know what to do.

639
00:34:46,820 --> 00:34:49,560
But if you want to know how important submarine school is,

640
00:34:49,660 --> 00:34:52,520
look at what happens to a navy that doesn't have one.

641
00:34:52,620 --> 00:34:54,360
That's the Russians.

642
00:34:54,460 --> 00:34:56,340
And if you look at the number of Russians

643
00:34:56,440 --> 00:35:00,100
who have been horribly mutilated by making mistakes

644
00:35:00,200 --> 00:35:03,000
on nuclear subs, you would tend to think that Groton

645
00:35:03,100 --> 00:35:07,740
is a real good investment for an awful lot of Americans.

646
00:35:07,840 --> 00:35:10,240
One of the most critical parts of submarine training

647
00:35:10,340 --> 00:35:11,780
is mastering firefighting.

648
00:35:14,420 --> 00:35:17,080
An underwater fire, if not handled properly,

649
00:35:17,081 --> 00:35:20,560
can mean certain death for the crew.

650
00:35:20,561 --> 00:35:22,920
It consumes oxygen very, very quickly.

651
00:35:22,921 --> 00:35:25,060
If the fire spreads, it will consume oxygen quicker

652
00:35:25,061 --> 00:35:28,060
than the boat can replenish it, and you'll all asphyxiate.

653
00:35:28,061 --> 00:35:30,660
So putting out that fire fast is absolutely critical.

654
00:35:34,940 --> 00:35:38,420
Trainees also repeatedly practice isolating, containing,

655
00:35:38,421 --> 00:35:41,620
and halting leaks in flood control drills.

656
00:35:41,621 --> 00:35:43,540
That very deep depth, a very small leak

657
00:35:43,541 --> 00:35:47,340
is a big problem because it sprays water everywhere.

658
00:35:47,341 --> 00:35:49,540
And it can cut a man in half.

659
00:35:49,541 --> 00:35:52,240
And you have to deal very quickly

660
00:35:52,241 --> 00:35:55,240
because you're talking about a matter of 30 seconds

661
00:35:55,241 --> 00:35:58,920
to a minute for a serious leak before it's completely out

662
00:35:58,921 --> 00:36:01,040
of hand and there's nothing you can do about it.

663
00:36:03,780 --> 00:36:07,060
It also sprays salt water all over all the electrical

664
00:36:07,061 --> 00:36:09,200
equipment that happens to be in that compartment.

665
00:36:09,201 --> 00:36:13,040
So if that's essential equipment, you've lost it.

666
00:36:13,540 --> 00:36:17,840
Enlistees practice repeatedly at the several dive and drive

667
00:36:17,940 --> 00:36:22,020
simulators that mimic the submarine's controls.

668
00:36:22,120 --> 00:36:26,020
They face extreme conditions, such as avoiding depth charges,

669
00:36:26,120 --> 00:36:30,760
steep ascents, and tailing enemy subs.

670
00:36:30,860 --> 00:36:34,720
The trainees also perform escape drills, which simulate the

671
00:36:34,820 --> 00:36:36,800
conditions of a sinking sub.

672
00:36:36,900 --> 00:36:40,040
They have to be drilled constantly in these procedures,

673
00:36:40,140 --> 00:36:42,360
so the reaction is almost a knee jerk.

674
00:36:42,361 --> 00:36:45,860
almost a knee jerk. It's almost intuitive how to compensate for

675
00:36:45,861 --> 00:36:50,520
anything that takes place. After one year of land study,

676
00:36:50,521 --> 00:36:55,220
enlistees then train on subs at sea for at least eight months to get their

677
00:36:55,221 --> 00:36:59,660
dolphins, the insignia proving full crew membership.

678
00:36:59,661 --> 00:37:03,260
Red Crown, this is Bravo 7 India. Go ahead, over.

679
00:37:03,261 --> 00:37:07,460
Those seeking promotion to officer often return to sub school several times

680
00:37:07,461 --> 00:37:10,060
over a five-year period between patrols at sea.

681
00:37:13,520 --> 00:37:16,600
The US relied heavily on the strict training of its crews,

682
00:37:16,601 --> 00:37:19,720
safety restrictions, and advanced technology

683
00:37:19,721 --> 00:37:23,320
to compete with a growing number of Soviet nuclear subs, which

684
00:37:23,321 --> 00:37:28,760
was about 10% more than the US by the mid-1970s.

685
00:37:28,761 --> 00:37:30,680
Technically, Soviet subs were also

686
00:37:30,681 --> 00:37:32,360
reaching near parity with the US.

687
00:37:32,361 --> 00:37:38,300
The Akula, a Soviet attack sub launched in 1975,

688
00:37:38,400 --> 00:37:41,140
attained speeds of 35 knots submerged

689
00:37:41,240 --> 00:37:43,140
and was extremely quiet.

690
00:37:43,240 --> 00:37:48,340
The underwater Cold War began to heat up all over the globe.

691
00:37:48,440 --> 00:37:52,020
That certain US Navy submarines would penetrate

692
00:37:52,120 --> 00:37:54,480
into Soviet territorial waters on a regular basis

693
00:37:54,580 --> 00:37:58,020
to conduct inshore intelligence gathering operations.

694
00:37:58,120 --> 00:38:01,060
We regularly shadowed Soviet fleet operations

695
00:38:01,061 --> 00:38:03,700
and would go directly underneath their ships to take what they

696
00:38:03,800 --> 00:38:04,700
call hull shots.

697
00:38:04,800 --> 00:38:07,000
There was photographs of the bottom of a Russian ship.

698
00:38:07,100 --> 00:38:11,400
They were on wartime footing all the time.

699
00:38:11,500 --> 00:38:14,700
The mission of the US attack subs was to operate on the far

700
00:38:14,800 --> 00:38:18,740
fringes of the Cold War, to confront Soviet subs, shadow

701
00:38:18,840 --> 00:38:22,720
enemy ships, and collect intelligence, sometimes in

702
00:38:22,820 --> 00:38:24,920
Soviet ports.

703
00:38:25,020 --> 00:38:28,160
They could operate alone and unsupported in waters

704
00:38:28,260 --> 00:38:30,120
controlled by the enemy.

705
00:38:30,121 --> 00:38:32,660
I used to tell my skippers, if you get where you're going

706
00:38:32,760 --> 00:38:34,600
and you're not outnumbered and alone,

707
00:38:34,700 --> 00:38:36,800
you're in the wrong place.

708
00:38:36,900 --> 00:38:41,840
And they could do that and stay there for long periods of time

709
00:38:41,940 --> 00:38:47,100
and collect signals intelligence and visual intelligence.

710
00:38:47,200 --> 00:38:49,900
The fast attack boats scared the crap out of people

711
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:52,380
for the simple reason that they could get there

712
00:38:52,480 --> 00:38:54,420
without being seen and kill people

713
00:38:54,520 --> 00:38:56,440
without being endangered themselves.

714
00:38:56,540 --> 00:39:00,120
Once upon a time in 1980s, we wanted to send them

715
00:39:00,121 --> 00:39:02,520
to the Russians.

716
00:39:02,620 --> 00:39:05,720
And so every U.S. Navy submarine that was at sea,

717
00:39:05,820 --> 00:39:09,120
on patrol, and was trailing a Soviet ship,

718
00:39:09,220 --> 00:39:13,260
at the same moment, globally, they activated their active radar

719
00:39:13,360 --> 00:39:15,160
and pinged their targets.

720
00:39:15,260 --> 00:39:16,880
We were trying to send the message to the Russians

721
00:39:16,980 --> 00:39:19,280
that we're here and we know where you are all the time,

722
00:39:19,380 --> 00:39:22,480
and there's nothing you can do about it.

723
00:39:22,580 --> 00:39:24,480
The deadly game of blind man's bluff

724
00:39:24,580 --> 00:39:26,620
continued in the pitch black recesses

725
00:39:26,720 --> 00:39:28,980
of the world's oceans.

726
00:39:28,981 --> 00:39:33,720
Then in 1982, the Soviet Union unveiled a true monster of the

727
00:39:33,820 --> 00:39:39,720
deep, the Typhoon, a 568-foot-long, 25,000-ton

728
00:39:39,820 --> 00:39:45,100
sub with 20 ballistic missiles carrying MIRV warheads with a

729
00:39:45,200 --> 00:39:50,240
range of 6,000 miles. It came with dual hulls, two

730
00:39:50,340 --> 00:39:54,940
reactors, and 24 torpedoes. It remains the largest submarine

731
00:39:54,941 --> 00:39:58,940
submarine ever built and is still operational.

732
00:39:59,040 --> 00:40:01,280
Because of its long-distance missile range and the

733
00:40:01,380 --> 00:40:04,440
effectiveness of US sonar technology in locating Soviet

734
00:40:04,540 --> 00:40:08,080
subs, the Russians retreated into an Arctic bastion

735
00:40:08,180 --> 00:40:12,400
strategy in the 1980s. They placed their ballistic

736
00:40:12,500 --> 00:40:16,500
missile subs under the Arctic ice near their home ports and

737
00:40:16,600 --> 00:40:19,100
surrounded them with attack subs.

738
00:40:19,200 --> 00:40:22,040
The typhoon's flat-top sail design reflected the new

739
00:40:22,140 --> 00:40:23,640
tactics.

740
00:40:23,641 --> 00:40:26,540
It needed to pop through the ice rather quickly,

741
00:40:26,640 --> 00:40:30,440
so it needed that kind of heavy squat durability

742
00:40:30,540 --> 00:40:32,640
so it could come up through the ice effectively

743
00:40:32,740 --> 00:40:37,580
to communicate and to launch its missiles.

744
00:40:37,680 --> 00:40:40,500
Since the Cold War ended in the late 1980s,

745
00:40:40,600 --> 00:40:44,300
US submarines are switching from a dual superpower strategy

746
00:40:44,400 --> 00:40:49,300
to more complex tactics to cope with a changing world.

747
00:40:49,400 --> 00:40:53,640
The real mission questions now are for the attack boats.

748
00:40:53,641 --> 00:40:57,280
They're going through a complete redefinition of what their world is about.

749
00:40:58,020 --> 00:41:00,740
They're being tightly linked to battle groups now, and they never were before.

750
00:41:00,980 --> 00:41:02,600
There is no longer a monolithic threat.

751
00:41:03,240 --> 00:41:06,520
The threat can come from anywhere, from an established country or a terrorist group,

752
00:41:06,740 --> 00:41:10,060
and more often than not, it's going to come in shallow water, not in deep water,

753
00:41:10,280 --> 00:41:13,060
because the countries we're talking about don't have deep water capability.

754
00:41:15,340 --> 00:41:18,375
Due to strategic arms limitation agreements, the U.S.

755
00:41:18,376 --> 00:41:20,380
and Russia have halted construction

756
00:41:20,381 --> 00:41:25,380
boomer subs and decommissioned several others.

757
00:41:25,480 --> 00:41:29,780
Both sides continue to build and improve their attack subs.

758
00:41:29,880 --> 00:41:33,120
Britain, France, and China also currently operate their own

759
00:41:33,220 --> 00:41:36,720
nuclear submarines. But after decades of engineering

760
00:41:36,820 --> 00:41:40,740
deadlines, churning production goals, and a constant crisis

761
00:41:40,840 --> 00:41:45,140
atmosphere, the US and Russian nuclear submarine programs have

762
00:41:45,240 --> 00:41:49,840
stepped back to a standoff. Though US subs carry better

763
00:41:49,841 --> 00:41:53,340
trained crews and more advanced sonar capabilities.

764
00:41:53,440 --> 00:41:56,740
The firepower on each side is enough to end humanity many

765
00:41:56,840 --> 00:42:00,580
times over. The financial costs have been

766
00:42:00,680 --> 00:42:03,580
staggering. From roughly mid-century, the

767
00:42:03,680 --> 00:42:07,400
U.S. has spent nearly $800 billion on its attack and boomer

768
00:42:07,500 --> 00:42:11,300
submarines. To many cold warriors, the money

769
00:42:11,400 --> 00:42:15,840
was well spent, at least so far. We didn't go to war.

770
00:42:15,940 --> 00:42:18,600
We never shot at them and they never shot at us.

771
00:42:18,601 --> 00:42:19,860
That's a real achievement.

772
00:42:19,960 --> 00:42:22,800
And the peace dividend in the mind of most people was,

773
00:42:22,900 --> 00:42:25,340
oh, boy, we can abolish the armed forces

774
00:42:25,440 --> 00:42:28,740
and spend all this money on lots of other things.

775
00:42:28,840 --> 00:42:32,340
The peace dividend was peace.

776
00:42:32,440 --> 00:42:34,340
Though hundreds of these lethal shapes

777
00:42:34,440 --> 00:42:37,580
have moved silently past each other in the oceans of the world

778
00:42:37,680 --> 00:42:42,600
for decades, no nuclear warhead has ever been launched.

779
00:42:42,700 --> 00:42:46,600
But every day, hundreds of feet under the ocean surface,

780
00:42:46,601 --> 00:42:50,600
A twilight cat and mouse game continues between these high-tech

781
00:42:50,601 --> 00:42:55,600
beasts, testing, probing, and shadowing each other for the time

782
00:42:55,601 --> 00:43:02,600
war may come, a war that would surely end human civilization.

